      <span class="label">Process capability on the top-performing style, well past the 1.67 six-sigma bar</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="status-box">
    <strong>Project Outcome</strong>
    Delivered a confirmed, statistically capable wind target for the plant's highest-volume
    tier and a validated regression equation and tiering framework for the rest of the
    product line, presented at Mohawk's summer intern expo alongside the business case for
    rollout.
  </div>

  <hr>

  <h2>Why This Mattered</h2>

  <p>
    A carpet roll is wound onto a fixed-diameter core inside a spool with a hard radius
    limit. Push a roll past that limit and it has to be split into two, which forces a seam,
    wastes yardage on both cut ends, and risks the kind of quality defect that turns into a
    customer claim. Every split also loses capacity at every downstream step: production
    throughput, transportation load efficiency, and warehouse storage density all take a
    hit. None of that shows up on a single roll's paperwork, which is exactly why a flat
    900 yard preset had survived as long as it had.
  </p>

  <p>
    The preset wasn't wrong so much as blind. Two styles wound to the identical 900 yards
    could sit at very different points on their radius budget: Style 83103 averaged an 18.9
    inch radius at 900 yards, leaving 2.6 inches of usable headroom and roughly 200 yards of
    length that was never being claimed. Style 83160, wound to the same 900 yards, averaged
    a 20.2 inch radius with only 1.4 inches of headroom left and under 100 yards of room to
    grow. One preset, two completely different amounts of money left on the table.
  </p>

  <hr>

  <h2>Data Collection</h2>

  <p>
    Fixing a blind preset starts with measuring what it was blind to. For each sampled roll,
    I measured two radii, at the 4 o'clock and 8 o'clock positions, with a tape from the edge
    of the cardboard core to the outer edge of the wound carpet. Roll numbers and averaged
    radii were recorded in Excel, then joined against Style, actual wound length, face
    weight, and the rest of the spec fields pulled from SEQUEL, the plant's production and
    specification database.
  </p>

  <div class="box-yellow">
    <strong>Why two readings per roll:</strong> a single radius reading is sensitive to how
    evenly a roll happens to be wound. Averaging two readings taken on opposite sides of the
    roll gave a more stable estimate of true wound radius before it went anywhere near a
    regression model.
  </div>

  <hr>

  <h2>Geometry First: The K-Constant</h2>

  <p>
    Before touching statistics, the ceiling on roll length is pure geometry: the annular area
    between the core radius and the maximum allowed outer radius, divided by the carpet's
    thickness, converted to yards. That relationship collapses to a single constant, 14.0717,
    multiplied by the inverse of the carpet's thickness:
  </p>

  <div class="box">
    Maximum Length (yd) = 14.0717 &divide; Thickness (in)
  </div>

  <p>
    That equation reframed the entire project. If the geometry is fixed, the only unknown
    left is thickness, and thickness is what actually varies style to style. Predicting
    maximum roll length is really just predicting thickness, which is a modeling problem
    Minitab could take on directly.
  </p>

  <p>
    An earlier attempt to fold this into a single "capacity factor" multiplier turned out to
    be the wrong approach. Working directly from the core geometry, &pi;(R<sub>o</sub>&sup2;
    &minus; R<sub>c</sub>&sup2;) &frasl; 144 with a fixed outer radius, was both more transparent
    to the team and easier to defend when someone asked where the number came from.
  </p>

  <hr>

  <h2>Sorting Styles Into Tiers</h2>

  <p>
    With inverse thickness (InvT) computed for every sampled style, the product line sorted
    naturally into six tiers. Each tier maps to a projected maximum length range, giving the
    floor a usable target well before a single-equation model was fully locked down.
  </p>

