  <p>
    Two styles with nearly identical inverse-thickness values still weren't guaranteed to
    behave alike in practice. That gap turned out to be driven by loop geometry under
    winding tension, not by anything visible on a spec sheet, which is part of why every
    tier still had to clear its own control-chart and capability check before a target was
    confirmed.
  </p>

  <hr>

  <h2>Proving Control Before Claiming Capability</h2>

  <p>
    A capability index is only meaningful if the process generating it is stable. Before any
    tier's target was confirmed, I ran I-MR (Individuals and Moving Range) charts in Minitab
    on its measured thickness data. A style with points outside its control limits, or an
    unstable moving range, went back for review before its Ppk or Cpk was trusted.
  </p>

  <div class="figure-grid">
    <figure>
      <img src="images/mohawk-imr-80665.png" alt="I-MR control chart for Style 80665 thickness measurements">
      <figcaption>Style 80665 (Tier 4): both the individuals and moving-range charts sit comfortably inside their control limits across all ten observations, confirming the process was in statistical control before its capability was assessed.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img src="images/mohawk-imr-88923.png" alt="I-MR control chart for Style 88923 thickness measurements flagging an out-of-control point">
      <figcaption>Style 88923 (Tier 3): observation 4 breaks the moving-range upper control limit, flagged in red by Minitab. A result like this gets investigated, not averaged away, before a target is set on that style.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <p>
    Once a style's process was confirmed in control, Minitab's process capability report gave
    Ppk and Cpk against an upper spec limit derived from the same 14.0717 constant divided by
    the target yardage. A style needed Ppk and Cpk above 1.33 to be called capable, and above
    1.67 to be called six-sigma capable. Ppk is the more conservative of the two, since it
    weighs overall roll-to-roll variation rather than only short-term, within-run scatter.
  </p>

  <div class="figure-grid">
    <figure>
