      <figcaption>Process capability report for Style 80519, one of the confirmed Tier 4 styles: Ppk and Cpk both above 2.1, with zero observed or expected parts per million beyond the upper spec limit.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <hr>

  <h2>Refining the Predictive Equation</h2>

  <p>
    Tiering got the floor a usable answer quickly, but the goal was a single equation that
    could predict thickness, and therefore maximum length, for a style without waiting on a
    full sample. Face weight was the dominant predictor, but two styles with identical face
    weight could still wind to noticeably different lengths. The variable that separated
    them was stitches per inch: a denser stitch compresses into a thinner, tighter pile, so
    it needed to enter the equation as its own term rather than getting absorbed into face
    weight.
  </p>

  <div class="box">
    <strong>Example: Style 83103</strong> (Face Weight = 14.0, Stitches/3in = 30.6)<br><br>
    Inv_Thickness = 101.1 &minus; 1.70(14.0) &minus; 0.0412(14.0&sup2;) + 1.181(10.2) = 81.389<br>
    Maximum Theoretical Length = 81.389 &times; 14.0717 = <strong>1,145 yd</strong><br>
    90% Confidence Target = <strong>1,053 yd</strong>
  </div>

  <p>
    That gap between the theoretical maximum and the 90% confidence target is deliberate.
    Publishing the theoretical ceiling as a running wind target would put half of all rolls
    over the radius limit by definition. Backing off to a confidence-bound target, rather
    than an arbitrary flat margin, was the difference between a number the floor could
    actually run against and a number that looked impressive on a slide.
  </p>

  <p>
    A Chow test across the sampled styles showed that a single equation form fit both PF
    and woven backing constructions equally well (p = 0.103), meaning the plant didn't need
    to maintain and defend two separate equations to get a model that held up across its
    product mix.
  </p>

  <hr>

  <h2>Confirmed Targets and the Business Case</h2>

  <p>
    The headline result presented at the intern expo was a confirmed Tier 4 wind target of
    1,020 yards, up from the generic 900 yard preset, backed by a Ppk of 2.04 on Style 80665,
    the best-performing style in that tier. That's not a projection; it's a target the
    process had already been shown capable of holding.
  </p>

  <p>
    The case for rolling that out plant-wide comes down to what a split actually costs.
    Every forced split creates a seam and wastes yardage on both resulting pieces, and every
    split also removes capacity from production throughput, trailer loading efficiency, and
    warehouse storage density, none of which show up on the roll's own paperwork. Recovering
    even a fraction of the headroom left in the old 900 yard preset, at the volume a tufting
    plant runs, compounds into a real reduction in scrap and a real gain in how consistently
    rolls process and store.
  </p>

