  <hr>

  <h2>What I'd Carry Forward</h2>

  <p>
    Spec-sheet variables alone have a real predictive ceiling on this kind of data, probably
    somewhere around 40 to 50 percent of the total variance within standard stock. The
    remainder is genuine textural difference between styles that never makes it onto a spec
    sheet, and the clearest path past that ceiling is physical caliper measurement on a
    per-style basis rather than another round of regression on the same columns. Flagging
    that limit clearly, instead of overselling the model's reach, was as much a part of the
    deliverable as the equation itself.
  </p>

  <p>
    I also learned to defend a functional form with evidence rather than deference. My
    supervisor's original equation used an exponential decay form; running a Box-Cox
    transformation and comparing AIC/BIC across candidate forms in Minitab showed that form
    no longer fit the current data well, and made the case for the linear regression that
    replaced it far more convincing than an opinion would have.
  </p>

